SKELETON — READ THIS BLOCK, THEN DELETE THE FILE OR FILL IT IN.

Most modules must NOT have this file. 64 of 225 do. If this module ports no
third-party source, reproduces no third-party data, and studied no named
third-party implementation, delete it: the `Provenance:` line in README.md is
the whole record, and `zig build check-catalog` requires that line either way.

LINE 1 IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE GATE READS IT. It must be exactly one of:

    <name> — third-party attribution
    <name> — provenance note

They are not interchangeable, and picking the wrong one is a real mistake with
precedent: `brotli` shipped this file as a `provenance note` claiming its
vectors had been GENERATED with the C library as an oracle, when they were
byte-for-byte copies of upstream's corpus. It was an attribution all along and
had to be corrected on 2026-08-12.

  third-party attribution — third-party code was ported or translated, OR
      third-party data is reproduced whose notice must travel with it. This
      file then CARRIES A CONDITION naming someone other than the zig-libs
      authors, and the module MUST also appear in the root NOTICE section 1.
      State the upstream project, its license, exactly what was taken, and
      whether it is source or data.

  provenance note — a named third-party implementation was studied for
      behavior, algorithm or API shape, and nothing was copied. This file
      carries NO condition and the module MUST NOT appear in root section 1.
      State the upstream project and its license anyway: that is the record of
      whose terms would apply if a line were ever copied from it later.

If in doubt, it is an attribution. The failure that matters is understating
what was taken, never overstating it.

Deciding between them is a question about what was actually done, so answer it
from the diff, not from memory. "We only looked at it" is what everyone says.
